Load rendering throttle, part 3: never lose a latched rendering update.

User-visible regression on builds 465/466 (reddit.com, onche.org): pages
intermittently stop repainting until the user interacts with the UI.
Root cause: WebCore latches its rendering-update request
(Page::m_renderingUpdateIsScheduled, set in scheduleRenderingUpdateInternal,
cleared only when Page::updateRendering runs) and asks the LayerTreeHost
exactly ONCE. The LayerTreeHost pipeline has interleavings that consume
that single wakeup without running the update - most directly
renderingUpdateRunLoopObserverFired()'s silent `if (m_isWaitingForRenderer)
return`, plus composition-done paths that only re-arm from the
m_scheduledWhileWaitingForRenderer flag. Stock code gets away with the
races because requests re-arrive within a frame; the load throttle's
deferral windows (250 ms) widen them into a permanent freeze: the one
request is dropped, the latch stays set, WebCore never asks again.

Fix:
 - LayerTreeHost tracks m_renderingUpdatePending (set in
   scheduleRenderingUpdate, cleared when updateRendering actually runs).
 - The observer's waiting-drop now sets m_scheduledWhileWaitingForRenderer
   so the existing composition-done recovery re-schedules it.
 - The composition-done recovery also re-arms from m_renderingUpdatePending
   (covers flag-consumed interleavings).
 - Bound the throttle window: WEBKIT_LOAD_RENDERING_THROTTLE_MAX_MS
   (default 10000) hard-caps how long the reduced cadence may stay engaged,
   so never-settling progress trackers (live feeds, hanging subresources -
   exactly reddit/onche) cannot keep a page at 4 Hz indefinitely.

Applies ON TOP of webkit-load-rendering-throttle-env.patch and
webkit-load-rendering-throttle-layertreehost.patch.

--- a/Source/WebCore/page/Page.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/page/Page.cpp
